e chow from T3 T4 T5 by drawing T2.  \n\nIf I discard B5, then my pair could be T3 or T4 or T5.  \n\nGiven opponents' discards:  \nPrev player: played B8, W6, B2, J3, etc.  \nNext player: played T6, W6, T9, W1, etc.  \n\nT2 is still relatively safe (3 left, not discarded much).  \n\n**Promising moves:**  \n1. **Play B5** \u2013 reduces Dots, keeps Bamboo concentration; pairs can be T3/T4/T5.  \n2. **Play T4** \u2013 keeps B5 for possible Mixed Double Chow with something? Not likely.  \n3. **Play T3** \u2013 keeps more flexibility.  \n\nThe best is to keep Bamboo shape for possible Half Flush? But W meld already breaks Half Flush (W is Characters). So Half Flush impossible.  \n\nI should aim for **All Chows** + **All Simples** + maybe **Short Straight** + **No Honors** = 2+1+1+1 = 5 fan, need 3 more. Possibly **Closed Wait** or **Self-Drawn** later.  \n\nDiscard B5 because it\u2019s isolated (only one B5, and B suit only appears in one meld already). Keeping all Bamboo increases chance of Bamboo-based patterns (Pure Straight not possible).  \n\nSo **Play B5** is reasonable.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nDiscarding B5 removes an isolated tile from a suit that\u2019s already represented by a completed meld (B7B8B9), allowing me to focus on developing my Bamboo sequences. Keeping T3, T4, T5, T6 together maximizes potential chows and possible pung (T6) for flexibility. With many T2 and T7 still available, completing a chow from T3 T4 T5 is likely. This keeps the hand simple and on track for All Chows and All Simples patterns while maintaining safe discards.\n\n**Answer:** Play B5"}
